<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baracoa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baracoa is a town on the north-eastern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, founded in 1511 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-velazquez-de-cuellar">Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar</a> as the first Spanish settlement on the island. It served briefly as the colony's capital before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de Cuba replaced it. Isolated by mountains, it remained a small port for centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baracoa.jpg" alt="Baracoa" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Postiaux · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baracoa_-_El_Yunque.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Baracoa, whose full original name is Nuestra Señora de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a> de Baracoa, lies near the eastern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> on the Bay of Honey, which is Bahía de Miel. A wide mountain range, including the Sierra del Purial, surrounds it and kept it isolated: until the 1960s the only access was by sea, when a 120-kilometre mountain road called La Farola was built from Guantánamo. The name is thought to come from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> word meaning the presence of the sea. The nearby table mountain of El Yunque shelters several species of ferns and palms found nowhere else.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The original inhabitants of the island included the Guanahatabey, Ciboney and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a>, who were almost wiped out by European diseases. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatuey">Hatuey</a>, who fled the Spanish on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> and raised an army against them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, is remembered as a local hero. According to the story, he was betrayed and sentenced to burn at the stake, and when a priest offered him salvation he asked whether the Spanish dead went to heaven, and said that he would rather go to hell.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> on 27 November 1492 at a place he named Porto Santo, generally taken from his description to be Baracoa, though Gibara has also been claimed. Tradition says he set up a cross, the Cruz de la Parra, on the sand. About 15 August 1511, the official foundation day, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-velazquez-de-cuellar">Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar</a>, first governor of Cuba, built a town here, making it the island's first capital. In 1518 it received the title of city and the first Cuban bishop was appointed there. Spanish fortifications, El Castillo, Matachín and La Punta, and a cemetery remain.</p><p>In the 17th and 18th centuries its isolation made Baracoa a haven for illegal trade with French and British merchants. In 1744, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-jenkins-ear">War of Jenkins' Ear</a>, the officer Luis de Unzaga, as chief commander, used a network of Irish merchants and French military personnel to be warned of British attacks, which kept the town at peace and let trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> prosper. Early in the 19th century refugees from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a> settled and began growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and cocoa. From the middle of that century, expeditions of independence fighters, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-maceo">Antonio Maceo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-marti">José Martí</a>, landed here.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Baracoa is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>'s main chocolate-manufacturing area, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> grown under royal palms by the rivers Miel, Toa and Yumuri, as well as bananas and coconuts. The Catedral Nuestra Señora de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a> holds the Cruz de la Parra. Carbon dating places it at about the period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>, but it is made of a local wood, so part of the tradition cannot be right.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baracoa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In eastern Cuba, around El Caney, Yateras and <b>Baracoa</b>, observers from the 19th century onwards recorded Indigenous communities, and some have kept a Taíno identity into the 21st century.</blockquote><a href="/culture/taino">Taíno · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar led the conquest of Cuba from 1511, founding <b>Baracoa</b> and other towns; the Taíno chief Hatuey, who resisted, was burnt in 1512.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-cuba">Captaincy General of Cuba</a></li></ul></section>
